## Management of active diabetic foot problems

Active foot problems require referral to a higher level of care. Best results in prevention of amputations have been achieved in settings where multidisciplinary facilities are available.

## When to refer

Patients with a foot ulcer should be referred to a more specialized level for further evaluation if management by a trained professional and the necessary equipment and consumables are not available at the primary care level. Patients with gross foot deformities and/or absent peripheral pulses, and patients with suspected lower limb ischaemia will need to be referred to a higher level of care.

Urgent referral to acute services is recommended for patients with any of the following:

- · infected ulcer
- · spreading infection
- · critical limb ischaemia
- · gangrene
- · suspicion of acute Charcot arthropathy
- · unexplained red swollen foot.